UNIT deburr
KIND FUNCTIONAL
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
# Pure string utility. No clock/RNG/env/fs/net seam. No injection point. Nothing to quarantine.

SIG deburr(string = '') -> string
# Default export of lodash.deburr@4.1.0:  module.exports = deburr
# The blind re-emitter MUST emit the function as the DEFAULT export:  export default deburr
# Harness calls: deburr(...args), where each oracle tuple is an args array, e.g. ['déjà vu'], [null], [-0].
# Called with zero args, `string` is undefined -> coerced to '' (see COERCION) -> returns ''.

DEPENDS-ON none
# Zero runtime dependencies. The deburredLetters map below is INLINE ALGORITHMIC DATA (a literal object
# in the source), NOT carried published-authority data. It is transcribed in full in this SIR and is part
# of the spec. There is no carried.json for this unit.

############################################################################################################
# BEHAVIOR — exact ordered pipeline
############################################################################################################
# The whole function body is exactly two steps:
#
#   function deburr(string) {
#     string = toString(string);                                      # STEP 1: coerce
#     return string && string.replace(reLatin, deburrLetter)          # STEP 2a: map Latin letters
#                            .replace(reComboMark, '');                # STEP 2b: strip combining marks
#   }
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STEP 1 — COERCION  (lodash toString / baseToString semantics, transcribed exactly)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   toString(value):
#     if (value == null) return '';        # loose equality: catches BOTH null AND undefined -> ''
#     else return baseToString(value)
#
#   baseToString(value):
#     if (typeof value == 'string') return value;          # strings pass through unchanged
#     if (isSymbol(value)) {                               # Symbol primitive OR Symbol object
#         return symbolToString ? symbolToString.call(value) : '';   # i.e. String(symbol) -> "Symbol(desc)"
#     }
#     var result = (value + '');                           # default JS string coercion
#     return (result == '0' && (1 / value) == -INFINITY) ? '-0' : result;   # PRESERVE sign of -0
#
#   isSymbol(value): typeof value == 'symbol'
#                    || (value is object-like && Object.prototype.toString.call(value) == '[object Symbol]')
#
# Coercion results that the oracle WILL exercise (all verified by running the real package):
#   null        -> ''                          undefined / no arg -> ''
#   ''          -> ''                           'abc'      -> 'abc'
#   0           -> '0'                          -0         -> '-0'      # the special -0 branch
#   123         -> '123'                        true       -> 'true'    false -> 'false'
#   [1,2,3]     -> '1,2,3'                       [[1],[2,[3]]] -> '1,2,3'   # nested arrays flatten via Array#toString
#   ['é','à']   -> 'e,a'      (coerced to 'é,à' FIRST, then deburred -> 'e,a')
#   []          -> ''                           {}         -> '[object Object]'
#   new Map()   -> '[object Map]'               new Set()  -> '[object Set]'
#   Uint8Array([233,224]) -> '233,224'          42n (bigint) -> '42'
#   new String('café') -> 'café' -> deburred 'cafe'      new Number(5) -> '5'
#
#   OUT-OF-ENVELOPE coercion (correct per spec, but NOT emitted by the generator because the input value does
#   not survive the oracle JSON codec round-trip; a blind re-emitter still reconstructs these from this spec):
#     NaN          -> 'NaN'         (number NaN does not round-trip JSON)
#     Infinity     -> 'Infinity'    -Infinity -> '-Infinity'   (do not round-trip JSON)
#     Symbol('x')  -> 'Symbol(x)'   Symbol() -> 'Symbol()'     (Symbol does not round-trip codec)
#     function/Date/objects-with-custom-toString -> their String() form (do not round-trip codec)
#   These are documented for completeness; the generator does NOT emit them.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STEP 2 — the `string && ...` SHORT-CIRCUIT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   After coercion, evaluate `string && string.replace(...).replace(...)`.
#   If `string` is the empty string '' (falsy), the && short-circuits and RETURNS '' (the empty string itself).
#   Any non-empty string is truthy, so the two .replace() calls run.
#   NET EFFECT: deburr('') === '' and deburr(null)/deburr(undefined) === ''. Return type is always a string
#   for any input the generator emits. (The replace chain itself can only ever yield '' for an all-stripped
#   input, but that path goes through the replaces, not the short-circuit; both yield '' identically.)
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STEP 2a — Latin-letter mapping:  string.replace(reLatin, deburrLetter)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   reLatin = /[\xc0-\xd6\xd8-\xf6\xf8-\xffĀ-ſ]/g        # GLOBAL flag, NO unicode flag, NO ignoreCase
#   i.e. matches any single char in the union of ranges:
#       U+00C0..U+00D6   (À..Ö)            # note: U+00D7 (× MULTIPLICATION SIGN) is EXCLUDED by the gap
#       U+00D8..U+00F6   (Ø..ö)            # note: U+00F7 (÷ DIVISION SIGN)       is EXCLUDED by the gap
#       U+00F8..U+00FF   (ø..ÿ)
#       U+0100..U+017F   (Ā..ſ, the whole Latin Extended-A block)
#   For each matched single char, replace it with deburredLetters[char] (the deburrLetter accessor =
#   basePropertyOf(deburredLetters): returns deburredLetters[key], or undefined if absent — but EVERY char
#   reLatin matches IS a key, so the lookup always hits; see SELF-CONSISTENCY note below).
#   Replacement values are 1-or-2 (or 3, for "Ae"/"ss") basic-Latin chars; some are multi-char ('Ae','ss','IJ',
#   'Oe',"'n", 'th', 'ij', 'oe', 'ae'). Replacement output is NOT re-scanned by reLatin (single .replace pass).
#
#   IMPORTANT — chars in reLatin's ranges that are NOT in deburredLetters:
#     U+00D7 (×) and U+00F7 (÷) are the ONLY codepoints in the Latin-1 Supplement letter span that reLatin
#     deliberately skips (via the \xd8 and \xf8 lower-bounds). Every OTHER codepoint matched by reLatin has a
#     map entry. So in practice reLatin and deburredLetters' keyset are identical and every match resolves.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STEP 2b — combining-mark strip:  .replace(reComboMark, '')
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   reComboMark = RegExp('[\\u0300-\\u036f\\ufe20-\\ufe23\\u20d0-\\u20f0]', 'g')   # GLOBAL flag, no unicode flag
#   It is built from the composed source string:
#       rsComboMarksRange   = '\\u0300-\\u036f\\ufe20-\\ufe23'    # Combining Diacritical Marks + Combining Half Marks
#       rsComboSymbolsRange = '\\u20d0-\\u20f0'                   # Combining Diacritical Marks for Symbols
#       rsCombo = '[' + rsComboMarksRange + rsComboSymbolsRange + ']'
#   So reComboMark matches (and DELETES, replacing with '') any single char in the union of THREE ranges:
#       U+0300..U+036F   (Combining Diacritical Marks)
#       U+FE20..U+FE23   (Combining Half Marks)            # <-- BEYOND the naive U+0300-U+036F; included
#       U+20D0..U+20F0   (Combining Diacritical Marks for Symbols)   # <-- ALSO beyond; included
#   Each matched char is replaced with the empty string (removed). All occurrences removed (global).
#
#   Boundary behavior VERIFIED against the live package (codepoint just outside a range must NOT strip):
#       U+02FF strip? NO       U+0300 strip? YES      U+036F strip? YES      U+0370 strip? NO
#       U+FE1F strip? NO       U+FE20 strip? YES      U+FE23 strip? YES      U+FE24 strip? NO
#       U+20CF strip? NO       U+20D0 strip? YES      U+20F0 strip? YES      U+20F1 strip? NO
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ORDER of operations (load-bearing): reLatin map (2a) runs FIRST, THEN reComboMark strip (2b).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   The chain is  string.replace(reLatin, deburrLetter).replace(reComboMark, '')  — left to right.
#   Observationally the two steps are independent for in-scope inputs (mapped output contains only basic-Latin
#   chars, which reComboMark never matches; combining marks are never matched by reLatin), so a precomposed
#   accented letter that is in the map (e.g. 'à' U+00E0) maps to 'a' in 2a and is unaffected by 2b; a base
#   letter followed by a SEPARATE combining mark (e.g. 'a'+U+0300) is left by 2a and stripped by 2b. Both
#   orderings would yield the same result for in-scope inputs, but the spec order is map-then-strip. A
#   re-emitter MUST apply BOTH replaces; emitting only one is a divergence.
#
#   NO .normalize() is called anywhere. deburr does NOT NFC/NFD-normalize the input. Decomposed sequences are
#   handled purely by the combining-mark strip; precomposed accented letters are handled purely by the map.
#   (VERIFIED: 'é' as decomposed U+0065 U+0301 -> 'e'; 'à' as precomposed U+00E0 -> 'a'. Both -> single 'a'/'e'.)

############################################################################################################
# deburredLetters MAP — COMPLETE, every key -> value, transcribed EXACTLY from source (verified by runtime
# enumeration of U+00C0..U+017F: 190 changed codepoints, matching this table exactly).
# Format:  U+XXXX -> 'value'
############################################################################################################
# --- Latin-1 Supplement block ---
# U+00C0 -> 'A'   U+00C1 -> 'A'   U+00C2 -> 'A'   U+00C3 -> 'A'   U+00C4 -> 'A'   U+00C5 -> 'A'
# U+00E0 -> 'a'   U+00E1 -> 'a'   U+00E2 -> 'a'   U+00E3 -> 'a'   U+00E4 -> 'a'   U+00E5 -> 'a'
# U+00C7 -> 'C'   U+00E7 -> 'c'
# U+00D0 -> 'D'   U+00F0 -> 'd'
# U+00C8 -> 'E'   U+00C9 -> 'E'   U+00CA -> 'E'   U+00CB -> 'E'
# U+00E8 -> 'e'   U+00E9 -> 'e'   U+00EA -> 'e'   U+00EB -> 'e'
# U+00CC -> 'I'   U+00CD -> 'I'   U+00CE -> 'I'   U+00CF -> 'I'
# U+00EC -> 'i'   U+00ED -> 'i'   U+00EE -> 'i'   U+00EF -> 'i'
# U+00D1 -> 'N'   U+00F1 -> 'n'
# U+00D2 -> 'O'   U+00D3 -> 'O'   U+00D4 -> 'O'   U+00D5 -> 'O'   U+00D6 -> 'O'   U+00D8 -> 'O'
# U+00F2 -> 'o'   U+00F3 -> 'o'   U+00F4 -> 'o'   U+00F5 -> 'o'   U+00F6 -> 'o'   U+00F8 -> 'o'
# U+00D9 -> 'U'   U+00DA -> 'U'   U+00DB -> 'U'   U+00DC -> 'U'
# U+00F9 -> 'u'   U+00FA -> 'u'   U+00FB -> 'u'   U+00FC -> 'u'
# U+00DD -> 'Y'   U+00FD -> 'y'   U+00FF -> 'y'
# U+00C6 -> 'Ae'  U+00E6 -> 'ae'
# U+00DE -> 'Th'  U+00FE -> 'th'
# U+00DF -> 'ss'
# --- Latin Extended-A block ---
# U+0100 -> 'A'   U+0102 -> 'A'   U+0104 -> 'A'
# U+0101 -> 'a'   U+0103 -> 'a'   U+0105 -> 'a'
# U+0106 -> 'C'   U+0108 -> 'C'   U+010A -> 'C'   U+010C -> 'C'
# U+0107 -> 'c'   U+0109 -> 'c'   U+010B -> 'c'   U+010D -> 'c'
# U+010E -> 'D'   U+0110 -> 'D'   U+010F -> 'd'   U+0111 -> 'd'
# U+0112 -> 'E'   U+0114 -> 'E'   U+0116 -> 'E'   U+0118 -> 'E'   U+011A -> 'E'
# U+0113 -> 'e'   U+0115 -> 'e'   U+0117 -> 'e'   U+0119 -> 'e'   U+011B -> 'e'
# U+011C -> 'G'   U+011E -> 'G'   U+0120 -> 'G'   U+0122 -> 'G'
# U+011D -> 'g'   U+011F -> 'g'   U+0121 -> 'g'   U+0123 -> 'g'
# U+0124 -> 'H'   U+0126 -> 'H'   U+0125 -> 'h'   U+0127 -> 'h'
# U+0128 -> 'I'   U+012A -> 'I'   U+012C -> 'I'   U+012E -> 'I'   U+0130 -> 'I'
# U+0129 -> 'i'   U+012B -> 'i'   U+012D -> 'i'   U+012F -> 'i'   U+0131 -> 'i'
# U+0134 -> 'J'   U+0135 -> 'j'
# U+0136 -> 'K'   U+0137 -> 'k'   U+0138 -> 'k'
# U+0139 -> 'L'   U+013B -> 'L'   U+013D -> 'L'   U+013F -> 'L'   U+0141 -> 'L'
# U+013A -> 'l'   U+013C -> 'l'   U+013E -> 'l'   U+0140 -> 'l'   U+0142 -> 'l'
# U+0143 -> 'N'   U+0145 -> 'N'   U+0147 -> 'N'   U+014A -> 'N'
# U+0144 -> 'n'   U+0146 -> 'n'   U+0148 -> 'n'   U+014B -> 'n'
# U+014C -> 'O'   U+014E -> 'O'   U+0150 -> 'O'
# U+014D -> 'o'   U+014F -> 'o'   U+0151 -> 'o'
# U+0154 -> 'R'   U+0156 -> 'R'   U+0158 -> 'R'
# U+0155 -> 'r'   U+0157 -> 'r'   U+0159 -> 'r'
# U+015A -> 'S'   U+015C -> 'S'   U+015E -> 'S'   U+0160 -> 'S'
# U+015B -> 's'   U+015D -> 's'   U+015F -> 's'   U+0161 -> 's'
# U+0162 -> 'T'   U+0164 -> 'T'   U+0166 -> 'T'
# U+0163 -> 't'   U+0165 -> 't'   U+0167 -> 't'
# U+0168 -> 'U'   U+016A -> 'U'   U+016C -> 'U'   U+016E -> 'U'   U+0170 -> 'U'   U+0172 -> 'U'
# U+0169 -> 'u'   U+016B -> 'u'   U+016D -> 'u'   U+016F -> 'u'   U+0171 -> 'u'   U+0173 -> 'u'
# U+0174 -> 'W'   U+0175 -> 'w'
# U+0176 -> 'Y'   U+0177 -> 'y'   U+0178 -> 'Y'
# U+0179 -> 'Z'   U+017B -> 'Z'   U+017D -> 'Z'
# U+017A -> 'z'   U+017C -> 'z'   U+017E -> 'z'
# U+0132 -> 'IJ'  U+0133 -> 'ij'
# U+0152 -> 'Oe'  U+0153 -> 'oe'
# U+0149 -> "'n"  U+017F -> 'ss'
#
# Notes that surprise: U+0149 (ŉ LATIN SMALL LETTER N PRECEDED BY APOSTROPHE) -> "'n" (apostrophe + n).
#                      U+017F (ſ LATIN SMALL LETTER LONG S) -> 'ss' (two s).
#                      U+00DF (ß) -> 'ss';  U+00DE/U+00FE (Þ/þ) -> 'Th'/'th';  U+00C6/U+00E6 (Æ/æ) -> 'Ae'/'ae'.
#                      The ligatures ﬁ/ﬀ/ﬂ (U+FB00 block) are NOT in the map and NOT in reLatin -> they pass
#                      through UNCHANGED. (The task hint claimed ﬁ->fi; that is FALSE for this version.)
############################################################################################################

############################################################################################################
# SELF-CONSISTENCY CROSS-CHECK (SIR <-> genInputs, both directions)
############################################################################################################
# Forward (every value genInputs can emit has defined behavior here):
#   - Every accented char the generator emits is U+00C0..U+017F: its mapping is in the table above (or is one
#     of U+00D7/U+00F7 which are explicitly excluded and pass through).
#   - Every combining mark the generator emits is in U+0300-U+036F / U+FE20-U+FE23 / U+20D0-U+20F0: stripped.
#   - The "just outside" boundary codepoints the generator emits (U+02FF, U+0370, U+FE1F, U+FE24, U+20CF,
#     U+20F1) are documented above as NON-stripping passthrough.
#   - Non-Latin passthrough (Cyrillic, CJK, Greek, emoji, ascii) is covered: reLatin/reComboMark match none.
#   - Coercion cases (null, undefined, numbers incl -0, bigint, booleans, arrays incl nested/empty, plain
#     object {}, Set, Map, Uint8Array, boxed String/Number) are each enumerated in STEP 1 above.
# Backward (generator never emits a value whose behavior is undefined here): it emits only codec-faithful
#   values whose behavior is in this SIR; non-round-trippable values (NaN/Infinity/Symbol/function/Date/
#   custom-toString) are documented OUT and are NOT emitted.

ORACLE value -> oracle.json
# Value-mode: harness runs deburr(...args) on original and each reconstruction, compares outputs via codec.

SCOPE
#   IN-CONTRACT LOGIC (reproduce exactly): the toString/baseToString coercion, the `string && ...` short
#     circuit, the two .replace() passes in order (reLatin->deburrLetter, then reComboMark->''), the exact
#     regexes/ranges/flags, and the complete deburredLetters map (inline algorithmic data, transcribed above).
#   CARRIED DATA: none. The deburredLetters map is inline algorithmic data, fully transcribed in this SIR;
#     it has no independent published authority table to attest against, so it is NOT carried-data — it is
#     part of the spec.
#   OUT-OF-CONTRACT: none (pure, no side effects, no stateful mutators, no external APIs).

PROVENANCE lodash.deburr@4.1.0 (npm), https://github.com/lodash/lodash, MIT license,
#   source file: node_modules/lodash.deburr/index.js (Custom Build, modularize exports="npm").
